{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788353107,"nonce":"HJ4YGtRUaWFk2l0N3aaYIWW/0h0=","title":"`prioritize` blocked on a DoBill work giver says WHAT is missing, never WHY","message":"Found while closing `eef837a`. Preserved rather than fixed there because it changes `prioritize`'s contract, not `bills`'.\n\nIn run `m1-20260901` the diagnostic that finally cornered the butcher defect was:\n\n```\nprioritize {pawn:91381, work:\"DoBillsButcherFlesh\", thing:103250}\n  -\u003e ok:true, rejected: [\"blocked: Missing 1x corpses\"]\n```\n\nThat string is the game's own `\"MissingMaterials\".Translate(text)` from `RimWorld/WorkGiver_DoBill.cs StartOrResumeBillJob`, where `text` is each unsatisfied `IngredientCount.Summary`. It is the best diagnostic in the bill surface and it is still only half an answer: **six corpses were on the map and it does not say why none of them counted.** The run read \"Missing 1x corpses\" beside a corpse on the bench's own cell and concluded the filter was broken. It was not — every corpse was either rotting (outside `ButcherCorpseFlesh.fixedIngredientFilter`'s `AllowRotten` exclusion) or forbidden.\n\n`eef837a` now answers that per BILL, in `bills` / `bill-add` / `bill-set`, as `ingredient_match.rejected` — the clause of `WorkGiver_DoBill`'s own predicate that rejected each candidate thing. `prioritize` does not carry it, and `prioritize` is the verb an agent reaches for when a specific pawn will not do a specific job.\n\n**Two things `prioritize` can say that `bills` structurally cannot**, which is why this is worth doing rather than telling the caller to go read `bills`:\n\n- `IsForbidden(Thing, Pawn)` rather than `IsForbidden(Thing, Faction)`. The pawn overload adds the ALLOWED AREA (`ForbidUtility.InAllowedArea`), the drafted / mental-state bypass in `CaresAboutForbidden`, and the lord checks. `bills` has no pawn and reports `forbidden_scope: \"faction\"` for exactly this reason. Mechanism F2 of the same run — \"a designation outside the allowed area does nothing\" — is this clause, and it cost a colonist.\n- `pawn.CanReserve(t)` and reachability. `BillIngredients.Scan` publishes both in `clauses_not_checked` and refuses to guess.\n\nScope: when `prioritize` refuses (or `ScanWorkGivers` produces a `blocked`) for a `WorkGiver_DoBill`-derived giver, attach the per-thing rejection breakdown for the bill it failed on, computed for THAT pawn. `BillIngredients.Scan` is the existing implementation and takes a `Bill`; it would need a pawn-scoped overload whose `forbidden` clause is the pawn overload and which additionally evaluates `CanReserve` and `CanReach`.\n\nHazards already catalogued in `BillIngredients.cs`'s header apply unchanged. `Bill.ShouldDoNow` still must not be called (it writes the scribed `paused`).\n\n\n1. `prioritize {pawn, work:\u003ca DoBill WorkGiverDef\u003e, thing:\u003cbench\u003e}` that comes back `blocked` carries, per bill it could not start, the same clause-named rejection breakdown `bills` publishes — with `forbidden` evaluated through `ForbidUtility.IsForbidden(Thing, Pawn)` and NOT the faction overload.\n2. The two clauses `bills` names in `clauses_not_checked` — `reachable` and `reservable` — are ANSWERED here, per candidate thing, and named as their own rejection reasons.\n3. A candidate outside the pawn's `Area_Allowed` is reported as such by name, distinctly from a `CompForbiddable`-forbidden one: they are the same `IsForbidden` for the game and different acts for the agent (`area {op:…}` versus `unforbid`).\n4. The game's own `blocked:` string is PRESERVED verbatim beside the breakdown. It is the game's answer and the breakdown is ours; a change that replaces it fails this item.\n5. `accept/` proves it against a bench: a corpse the pawn cannot reserve, one outside its allowed area, and one forbidden, each producing its own named reason in one `prioritize` call.","files":null}]}